<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=postage-stamp">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Postage stamp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Adhesive postage stamp, Penny Black</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A postage stamp is a small piece of paper sold by a postal administration to show that postage has been paid. The first adhesive postage stamp, the Penny Black, was issued in the United Kingdom on 1 May 1840 and valid from 6 May, as part of the postal reforms promoted by Sir Rowland Hill. It bore a profile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> drawn by Henry Corbould from a medal by William Wyon, and was printed by Perkins, Bacon &amp; Petch. The canton of Zürich issued stamps on 1 March 1843 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> on 1 August 1843, and by 1860 stamps were used in 90 countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/postage-stamp.jpg" alt="Postage stamp" width="330" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salvadorian government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salvador_1892_Sc74.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A public post was set up in 1837 and <b>postage stamps</b> under the Indian Postal Act of 1854; W. B. O'Shaughnessy's telegraph trial of 1851 grew to 4,555 miles of lines by 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1902 opponents of a Nicaraguan canal sent US senators Nicaraguan <b>postage stamps</b> showing Momotombo volcano in eruption; shortly afterwards the Senate chose Panama.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Tibet created its own postal service in 1912 and printed its first <b>postage stamps</b> in Lhasa that year, marked &quot;Tibet Government&quot; in Tibetan and &quot;Tibet&quot; in English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state set up a provisional government at Agios Kirykos, adopted its own flag and issued <b>postage stamps</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-icaria">Free State of Icaria</a></li><li><blockquote>Marie I issued a constitution, created orders of chivalry, designed a flag and printed <b>postage stamps</b>, which he sold to collectors.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sedang">Kingdom of Sedang</a></li><li><blockquote>The prince, usually a Phanariot Greek, governed with an elected assembly and his own administration, flag and later <b>postage stamps</b>, from Vathy.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-samos">Principality of Samos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1 May 1840</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Rowland Hill · <a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>In April 1680 William Dockwra established the London Penny Post, which used a hand stamp on the letter itself; many historians consider it the first postage stamp. In 1835 Lovrenc Košir of Ljubljana suggested pressed paper wafers as stamps, but the proposal was not adopted. Accounts vary as to who invented the adhesive stamp.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/papermaking">Papermaking<span>179–141 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=postage-stamp">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
